.in 5
.rm 65
.he "mem (4)                    10/20/26                       mem (4)"
.fo "                              -#-"
.bd
DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
mem
- core memory device
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
This device reads and writes kernel memory (addresses 0 to FFFF).
One must be exceedingly careful in protecting this device,
since there are several hardware registers in this address range
that will crash the system upon being read.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
LOW MEMORY
.in +5
.br
A few things the kernel wants other programs to find are left at
fixed low addresses.  A program seeks to one and reads it; the
lengths of a table's entries still come from the headers the
program is built with, but the table's address and size need not
be.

.in +10
.ti -10
0x1000		The kernel's entry point, a jump instruction.
.ti -10
0x1003		The address of the process table, a word.
.bd
ps
seeks here and reads the pointer.
.ti -10
0x1005		The address of the mount table, a word.
.bd
df
seeks here and reads the pointer.
.ti -10
0x3ea		The number of entries in the mount table, a byte.
The kernel writes it from its own NMOUNT at boot, so df and
kstat report the running kernel's size and are not rebuilt when
it changes.

.ti -10
0x3eb		The number of buffer cache headers, a byte, written
when the cache reaches its final size.  A pool of exactly 256
reads as zero; the pool never has fewer than eight, so zero
means 256.

.sp
The words at 0x1003 and 0x1005 are low byte first.  The
addresses are the kernel's own; the kernel's symbol table in
.bd
/micronix
is the general way to find everything else, and is what kstat
reads.
